To load module path
    source scripts/setup.sh

This brings Core modules to the fore. 

    ./scripts/02-openmpi.sh #Builds the openmpi module. 
    #Ultimately you get ~/modules/Compilter/gcc/13.3.0/openmpi/5.0.3 for instance

write_module has the structure
    write_module LEVEL NAME VERSION ROOT EXTRA

    LEVEL = core
    LEVEL = compiler
    LEVEL = mpi




Or simply (in a new shell)

    module purge
    source ~/scripts/setup.sh #Brings the Core modules to the fore.

Then:

    module avail

At this stage, you should only see the Core modules, for example:

gcc/13.3.0

You should not see OpenMPI yet.


    module load gcc/13.3.0


=================
--module-only skips the download/build/install steps and only rewrites the module.
--force removes the existing installation and rebuilds from scratch.
The module file is always written after a successful install or in module-only mode.

Now you can do all of these:

    ./01-gcc.sh

Configure GCC if needed and write the module.

    ./01-gcc.sh --module-only

Rewrite only the module file.

    ./01-gcc.sh --force

Recreate the installation (symlinks and version file) and rewrite the module.


| Package             | Depends on               | Module level |
| ------------------- | ------------------------ | ------------ |
| GCC                 | —                        | Core         |
| OpenMPI             | GCC                      | Compiler     |
| OpenBLAS            | GCC                      | Compiler     |
| FFTW                | GCC                      | Compiler     |
| LibXC               | GCC                      | Compiler     |
| **ScaLAPACK**       | GCC + OpenMPI + OpenBLAS | MPI          |
| **HDF5 (parallel)** | GCC + OpenMPI            | MPI          |
| Quantum ESPRESSO    | Everything above         | MPI          |


Core
└── gcc

Compiler
└── gcc/13.3.0
    ├── openmpi
    ├── openblas
    ├── fftw
    └── libxc

MPI
└── gcc/13.3.0
    └── openmpi/5.0.3
        ├── scalapack
        ├── hdf5
        └── qe